Security refactor upgrade
=========================

This is a runtime/schema/session migration, not a drop-in PHP 5 update. The legacy
Smarty UI and includes are replaced by native PHP controllers, rendering and DNS
conversion. Account/domain/record/default management, logs, AXFR, query and export
remain; old template customization must be ported. Review INSTALL first.

1. Put the application and publisher in maintenance mode. Back up the complete
   database, configuration and served data/data.cdb; test restoration. Install into
   a NEW directory so deleted legacy helpers/templates do not survive deployment.
   Preserve original application source separately for rollback, not in webroot.

2. Test a sanitized database copy with PHP 8.4+ and your actual MySQL/DNS versions.
   Review duplicate account emails and domain names case-insensitively; resolve
   duplicates deliberately before migration. Resolve NULL/invalid host/value
   fields, malformed names, out-of-zone owners, and TXT/SPF values over 255 bytes.
   SQL migration intentionally does not invent replacements for invalid DNS data.
   Review old MD5 accounts and require resets for dormant/privileged users.

3. Configure the new environment and server rules from INSTALL. Run
   `php bin/admin.php migrate` with a temporary DDL-capable identity while traffic
   is stopped. It converts tables to InnoDB/utf8mb4, widens password/name/value
   fields, adds missing SRV columns, adds unique email/domain indexes and new
   security tables, and revokes sessions/reset tokens. MySQL DDL is not atomic:
   on failure inspect the database and restore the backup before retrying if needed.
   Old active_sessions/php_sessions are unused; remove them after successful
   validation using your database retention procedure. Remove old session files.
   The shipped test@test.com/test credential is disabled. Provision a named admin
   using create-admin, or use `set-password EMAIL` with a private stdin password to
   recover an existing account. A successful legacy MD5 login upgrades its hash;
   new passwords/reset passwords use password_hash and password_verify.

4. Install the new publisher and Bearer credential before resuming publication.
   Old get_data IP-trust and unauthenticated URLs no longer work. axfr_get.php is
   retired; transfers run inside authenticated senior-admin requests with approved
   destination IPs. Arbitrary DNS destinations and recursive-query mode are gone.
   Imports validate the transferred records, reject unsupported/glue shorthand,
   and preserve the transferred SOA/NS rather than applying legacy override flags.
   Up to ten zones may be imported per request; each zone is committed separately.
   If a later zone fails, inspect the domain list before retrying the remainder.
   Query/transfer process limits are 20 seconds and 5 MiB each.

5. Old URL session bookmarks and GET mutation/credential requests are rejected.
   Cookie sessions require HTTPS. All modifying forms require POST plus CSRF.
   Existing sessions lose access on account deletion, deactivation, password/email
   changes and role/group changes. Users must sign in again after such changes.
   Group admins cannot inspect/manage accounts outside their own user group.

6. Run all INSTALL acceptance checks. In particular, check export of existing data
   before switching DNS publication: malformed rows now fail the whole export;
   SOA deletion is blocked, each active zone needs exactly one SOA, and automatic
   PTR needs matching reverse-zone ownership. Restore backups of both database
   and old code/config to roll back; the legacy runtime should remain isolated.

Historical logs may contain plaintext passwords and session URLs from the old
version. Restrict/rotate those logs, investigate exposure and rotate affected
credentials through your incident/retention procedures. This patch cannot remove
copies already retained by proxies, backups or external logging services.
